CCS delay model fixture
=======================

Provenance
----------

`asap7sc7p5t_INVBUF_RVT_FF_ccs.lib.gz` is the ASAP7 7.5-track INVBUF standard
cell library at the RVT / FF corner, trimmed to the two cells `chain.vg`
instantiates.

Taken from OpenROAD-flow-scripts, which ships the library gzipped (upstream ships
it as .7z, which nothing in this test suite can unpack):

    repo    https://github.com/The-OpenROAD-Project/OpenROAD-flow-scripts
    commit  f7b5958b22b1d93898a5861ea2749f62c664eaaa
            ("asap7: add ccs for RVT/FF corner for testing", 2024-05-09)
    path    flow/platforms/asap7/lib/CCS/asap7sc7p5t_INVBUF_RVT_FF_ccs_220122.lib.gz
    blob    5367152b03cb60068a116939a62928344712e89f

    sha256 of that file decompressed:
    e426a1e2bb871deebcb0f6a81fc902e504aff0fb660af7bde75ff3bed4807be9

ORFS in turn vendors it from the ASAP7 release it is named after:

    repo    https://github.com/The-OpenROAD-Project/asap7sc7p5t_28
    commit  d88477438935a5a388bd6294f682dc405c93c5d2 ("LIB/CCS/INVBUF", 2023-11-02)
    path    LIB/CCS/asap7sc7p5t_INVBUF_RVT_FF_ccs_220122.lib.7z

License: BSD 3-Clause, Copyright 2020 Lawrence T. Clark, Vinay Vashishtha, or
Arizona State University. The license text is kept verbatim in the file header.

What was changed
----------------

Every cell group except `BUFx2_ASAP7_75t_R` and `INVx1_ASAP7_75t_R` was deleted,
and the note at the top of the file was prepended. Nothing else was touched: the
library header, the lookup table templates and the two surviving cell groups
(NLDM tables, CCS `output_current` tables and `receiver_capacitance`) are the
original bytes. 2.4 MB and 37 cells became 136 KB and 2 cells.

    sha256 of this fixture decompressed:
    8c547b5c313d78f8b68e33899af408f9b4cd4ce946cdc85aa3eeea6a62bc25fa

To regenerate it, fetch the file named above and keep the header (everything
before the first `  cell (` line) plus those two `  cell (...) { ... }` groups plus
the closing brace.

Why it is here
--------------

No library SiliconCompiler can otherwise reach ships CCS timing data -- lambdapdk
carries NLDM only -- and without CCS data a test cannot tell
`set_delay_calculator prima` working from `prima` silently falling back to the
default calculator, which is what it does for every arc whose liberty has no CCS
waveforms.

The file carries both the NLDM tables and the CCS `output_current` tables, so a
test can read the *same* file under both delay models and attribute any
difference to the calculator alone.

`chain.typical.spef` is hand written, and it matters: prima needs a parasitic
*network* on the driver pin (it runs its own model order reduction, so it
declines a pre-reduced parasitic). With no parasitics annotated at all it falls
back to the default calculator and the two delay models produce identical
numbers.
